---
title: "Weights"
subtitle: "Survey weights, precision weights and population weights"
bibliography: [../references.bib, ../references-new.bib]
---



This module of the [companion curriculum](index.qmd) works on three cases, each of which weights a different kind of data. The immigration-and-trust case fits its claim on respondents of the European Social Survey (ESS), whose files carry a design weight, a post-stratification weight and a population weight, and shows what each makes a regression describe. The cohabitation case fits its claim on women of the National Survey of Family Growth (NSFG), a survey that samples some groups at higher rates than others and its women in clusters within strata, and shows both the weights and the errors that follow the design. The EU-frames case fits its claim on country-year means of Eurobarometer respondents, where weights enter at two levels, inside each cell and across the cells, and separates the three arguments that the word 'weighting' covers there.

## The immigration-and-trust case {#sec-trust}



The immigration-and-trust case takes its claim from a study by the original author (OA) of that case, and in this section OA refers to that author. The study relates concern about immigration to distrust of national politics among about 110,000 respondents of the first four rounds of the ESS in 16 Western European countries [@McLaren2012CulturalDivideEurope], and the paper says nothing about weights. The model is a regression on respondents, so a weight enters it directly. In a weighted least-squares fit each respondent counts in proportion to their weight, because the fit chooses the coefficients that minimise the sum of the squared residuals with each residual multiplied by the weight of its respondent, and the fit compares respondents only with others from their own country and round. Every number in this section is computed when the page is built from the extract that the course ships, `cases/trust/data/trust_ess.csv`, and from the files of the curve of the case, among the 110,732 respondents whom the paper analyses: the citizens of the 16 countries who do not belong to an ethnic minority. The [specification menu](../cases/trust/menu.qmd#weights) of the case sets out the weighting decision in brief.

### The three weights of the ESS {#sec-trust-ess-weights}

Every file of the ESS carries three weights, and the extract keeps all three. The **design weight** (`dweight`) corrects for sample designs that do not give every adult of a country the same chance of being selected, which leaves some kinds of people, such as those in larger households, under-represented in the unweighted sample. It is the inverse of the probability of selection, scaled so that it averages 1 in every country-round (to three decimals in the extract), and it makes each national sample stand for the adult population of its country. Where every adult had the same chance, the design weight is the same for everyone, and its design effect is 1.00 to two decimals in Belgium, Denmark, Finland, Norway and Sweden, and elsewhere it runs up to 1.41 in Portugal. Kish's design effect of a set of weights is the factor by which unequal weights inflate the variance of a weighted mean compared with an unweighted sample of the same size, $n \sum_i w_i^2 / \left(\sum_i w_i\right)^2$, so that a design effect of 1.25 leaves the precision of a sample four-fifths as large.

The **post-stratification weight** (`pspwght`) starts from the design weight and adjusts it further, so that the weighted sample of each country-round matches the population on age, sex, education and region. It also corrects part of what the design weight cannot reach, the imbalance that remains when some kinds of selected people take part less often than others. It describes the same national adult populations as the design weight, at a higher cost in precision, with design effects from 1.03 to 1.56.

The **population size weight** (`pweight`) is constant within each country-round. The ESS defines it as the population aged 15 and over divided by the number of respondents in the data file times 10,000, so multiplying it back gives the adult population that a country-round stands for: about 70.5 million in Germany and 3.3 million in Ireland, on average over their rounds. On its own it changes nothing within a country-round. Multiplied by the design weight or by the post-stratification weight, it makes each country-round count in proportion to its adult population rather than its number of interviews.

### Weights and estimands {#sec-trust-describe}

@tbl-trust-five fits the reference specification of the curve (distrust of parliament, the controls of the paper, every item answered and an intercept for every country-round) under the five ways of using the weights that the menu offers.


::: {#tbl-trust-five .cell tbl-cap='The reference specification of the immigration-and-trust case under each use of the weights, with errors clustered by country (t on 15 df). The shift is the change from the fit without weights. Sources: `cases/trust/data/spec_curve.csv` for the three options on the curve and `cases/trust/data/display_options.csv` for the two off it.'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

