:::


The coefficient moves by little, from 0.163 to 0.177, and the reason why it moves at all shows what each weight does. With an intercept for every country-round, the coefficient compares respondents only within their own country and round, and it can be written exactly as a weighted average of 16 slopes, one for each country. The slope of a country is the association between concern and distrust among its respondents once the controls are held constant. Its weight is the share of the variation in concern, net of the controls and of the intercepts, that the respondents of the country contribute, so a country counts for more when it has more respondents and when they differ more in their concern. @tbl-trust-shares gives the shares and the slopes without weights and with design and population weights, and in each column the shares multiplied by the slopes add up to the coefficient of @tbl-trust-five.


::: {#tbl-trust-shares .cell tbl-cap='The reference specification as a weighted average of one slope per country: the share of each country in the estimate and the slope among its respondents, without weights and with design and population weights, beside the adult population that the population weight assigns to the country (the mean over its rounds). Within each pair of columns, the sum of share × slope is the coefficient. Source: `cases/trust/data/trust_ess.csv`.'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

