The unweighted shares show the higher sampling rates. Black women made up 24.4% of the unions of 1990–1995 in the sample and 14.1% of the population of unions that the sample stands for, and 23.6% and 17.0% of those of 2005–2010; Hispanic women made up 13.6% and 10.2% of the earlier cohort. The weighted shares are the shares of Table 1 of the paper, which reports them to two decimals. The design effects are small in the 1995 survey, from 1.06 to 1.30, and large in the 2006–2010 survey, from 2.92 to 5.15, whose User's Guide warns that its "weights vary widely" (p. 13): a share of the later cohort has the precision of a simple random sample of about a third of its size or less. The design effect of an estimate combines the unequal weights with the clustering of the women, and the package computes it for each estimate rather than for the weights alone.

### A subpopulation of the survey {#sec-nsfg-domain}

The unions of the paper are a subpopulation of each survey, which survey statisticians call a domain, and the design was declared on every woman of the file before `subset()` kept them. The order matters. The User's Guide advises: "When estimating variances for population subgroups (such as those who have ever had sexual intercourse or those 30-44 years of age), it is important to use the full dataset to set up your analytic variables, and define your subgroup of interest within your procedure" (p. 16). The union file holds every woman of each survey whose first cohabitation came before any marriage, and every stratum and cluster of each survey has at least one of them, as its [codebook](../cases/cohabitation/data/cohab_unions_codebook.md) records, so the design declared on it carries every cluster of the two surveys, whereas a design declared on the 2,071 unions of the paper alone fails.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
co_wrong <- survey::svydesign(ids = ~psu, strata = ~stratum, weights = ~weight, nest = TRUE,
                              data = datawizard::data_filter(co_women, paper))
survey::svymean(~race, co_wrong)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-error}

```
Error in `onestrat()`:
! Stratum (2006-10_106) has only one PSU at stage 1
```


:::
:::


Some strata hold only one cluster with a union of the paper, and a stratum with a single cluster gives the package no way to estimate the variation between clusters within it. The option `options(survey.lonely.psu = "adjust")` makes the error go away by borrowing the variation from the other strata, but the standard errors that it gives are those of a different design. Declaring the design on the full file and keeping the domain with `subset()` lets the clusters without a union of the paper count in their strata with a contribution of zero, which is how the domain was sampled. The estimator of the case does the same on the person-months: it declares the design on every cluster of the two surveys, adds the months of the analysed unions and keeps them with `subset()`, as the [binary-outcomes module](binary-outcomes.qmd#sec-discrete-logit) shows.

### The weights in the model {#sec-nsfg-weights-model}

The curve of the case keeps the weighted and the unweighted fit as two estimands. At the reference settings, the multinomial logit of the paper with the age at the start, race-ethnicity and education held constant, the coefficient of the later cohort is −0.730 with the weights and −0.804 without them. Across the 72 pairs of twin specifications of the curve, which agree on every other decision, leaving out the weights moves the estimate by −0.049 on average, from −0.080 to −0.024, and changes no verdict.

The weights move the estimate because the change differed between the groups that the NSFG sampled at different rates. With an interaction of race-ethnicity and the period, as in Model 2 of the paper, the coefficient of the later cohort is −0.69 for non-Hispanic white women, −0.40 for Black women and −1.14 for Hispanic women. A model without that interaction averages the three, and without weights each group counts by its number in the sample rather than by its number in the population. The weighted coefficient describes the change in the population of cohabiting women aged 15–44 that each survey represents, and the unweighted one describes the change in the women whom the two surveys interviewed. In the immigration-and-trust case each weight changed whose association the coefficient averages, and the survey weight does the same here, because a regression on respondents describes the people who count in it. The [specification menu](../cases/cohabitation/menu.qmd) keeps both: the weighted fit as the estimand of the paper, the unweighted fit as the estimand of every analysis that used the file which Multi100 gave the analysts, since that file carried no weights.

### Errors that follow the design {#sec-nsfg-errors}

The person-months of one union belong to one couple, and the women were sampled in clusters, so neither the months nor the women are independent draws. @tbl-nsfg-errors sets the standard error of the reference specification under three rules: errors that follow the strata and clusters of both surveys, the rule that classes every point of the curve; errors clustered by woman, which treat the months of each union as a cluster and ignore the design of the survey; and the conventional errors of the fit, which treat every person-month as an independent observation.


::: {#tbl-nsfg-errors .cell tbl-cap='The reference specification of the cohabitation case under three rules for its standard error: the coefficient of the later cohort, its standard error, the test statistic and its degrees of freedom. Source: `cases/cohabitation/data/display_inference.csv`.'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

