The conventional standard error, 0.085, is about a quarter smaller than the design-based one, 0.111, which makes every test look stronger than the data allow. Clustering by woman, 0.108, comes close to it, because it allows for the months of a union belonging together, but it leaves out the strata and the clusters within which the women were sampled. The design-based test also takes its degrees of freedom from the design: 241, which the survey package counts from the clusters and strata of the two surveys, less the coefficients of the model, rather than from the 33,197 person-months. Here every rule finds a clear decline, but on a smaller sample or a weaker effect the choice between them can decide the verdict, which is why the curve classes every specification by the design-based errors and the menu keeps the other two off it.

